<!--
  NeurOne PostHog — ClickHouse user for the event-retention script
  NP-ANALYTICS-001 §7.1 and §7.7 (OI-PRIV-47, REQ-SW03-34 (e))
  Path: /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/np-retention-user.xml

  apply_event_retention.py reads each events table's DDL from system.tables and, with apply, changes its TTL. Its user may do
  those two things and nothing else: it cannot read, insert, delete or drop any row of analytics data. Before OI-PRIV-47 the
  script ran as PostHog's application user, which reads every table, so a retention run was indistinguishable in any log from
  a read of every installation's timeline. The separate-users rule of NP-ANALYTICS-001 §7.7 forbids that; the script now refuses
  to run as `posthog`.

  The password comes from the container's environment (RETENTION_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD, set in .env, required by the compose file
  so the user never exists with an empty password). The table name must match the script's tables option and the pinned PostHog commit's
  schema. NOT YET EXERCISED AGAINST A REAL STACK (OI-ANALYTICS-01 (2)): whether MODIFY TTL, which by default also materialises the
  TTL on existing parts, needs any grant beyond ALTER MODIFY TTL on ClickHouse 23.12 is to be confirmed by NP-MAN-OPS-001 §4.
-->
<clickhouse>
  <users>
    <np_retention>
      <password from_env="RETENTION_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD"/>
      <networks><ip>::/0</ip></networks>
      <profile>default</profile>
      <quota>default</quota>
      <grants>
        <query>GRANT SELECT(database, name, create_table_query) ON system.tables</query>
        <query>GRANT ALTER MODIFY TTL ON posthog.sharded_events</query>
      </grants>
    </np_retention>
  </users>
</clickhouse>
